Integrity Monitoring and Tamper Detection Tool
Prerequisites
Basic Linux command line knowledge (navigating directories, creating files)
Understanding of file permissions
Familiarity with text editors (nano or vi)
Access to a Linux machine with sudo privileges
Learning Objectives
By completing this lab, you will:

Understand file integrity monitoring concepts
Install and configure AIDE (Advanced Intrusion Detection Environment)
Monitor files for unauthorized changes
Detect tampering attempts on critical system files
Generate and interpret security alerts
Environment Setup
Step 1: Update System and Install AIDE
AIDE is an open-source file integrity monitoring tool that creates a database of file attributes and detects changes.

# Update package lists
sudo apt update

# Install AIDE
sudo apt install aide -y
Step 2: Verify Installation
# Check AIDE version
aide --version
Expected output should show AIDE version information.

Task 1: Initialize AIDE and Create Baseline Database
Step 1.1: Configure AIDE
AIDE's configuration file defines which directories and files to monitor.

# View the default configuration
sudo cat /etc/aide/aide.conf | grep -v "^#" | grep -v "^$" | head -20
The configuration file contains rules for monitoring different directories. Key sections include:

/bin - System binaries
/sbin - System administration binaries
/etc - Configuration files
Step 1.2: Create Initial Database
This creates a baseline of your system's current state.

# Initialize AIDE database (this may take 2-5 minutes)
sudo aideinit
Note: This command scans all configured files and creates a database of their attributes (checksums, permissions, timestamps).

Step 1.3: Move Database to Active Location
# Copy the newly created database to the active location
sudo cp /var/lib/aide/aide.db.new /var/lib/aide/aide.db
Step 1.4: Verify Database Creation
# Check database file exists
ls -lh /var/lib/aide/aide.db
You should see a database file with a size of several megabytes.

Task 2: Monitor Files and Detect Tampering
Step 2.1: Create Test Files to Monitor
Create a directory with files we'll monitor for changes.

# Create test directory
sudo mkdir -p /opt/critical_files

# Create test files
sudo bash -c 'echo "Original content - Configuration file" > /opt/critical_files/config.txt'
sudo bash -c 'echo "Original content - Data file" > /opt/critical_files/data.txt'
sudo bash -c 'echo "#!/bin/bash" > /opt/critical_files/script.sh'
sudo chmod +x /opt/critical_files/script.sh

# Verify files created
ls -la /opt/critical_files/
Step 2.2: Add Custom Monitoring Rule
Add our test directory to AIDE's configuration.

# Backup original configuration
sudo cp /etc/aide/aide.conf /etc/aide/aide.conf.backup

# Add custom monitoring rule
sudo bash -c 'echo "" >> /etc/aide/aide.conf'
sudo bash -c 'echo "# Custom monitoring for lab" >> /etc/aide/aide.conf'
sudo bash -c 'echo "/opt/critical_files R" >> /etc/aide/aide.conf'
Explanation: The R rule monitors for changes in permissions, ownership, file type, and content.

Step 2.3: Update Database with New Files
# Update AIDE database to include new files
sudo aide --update

# Replace old database with updated one
sudo cp /var/lib/aide/aide.db.new /var/lib/aide/aide.db
Step 2.4: Run Initial Check (Should Show No Changes)
# Perform integrity check
sudo aide --check
Expected output: "AIDE found NO differences between database and filesystem."

Step 2.5: Simulate Tampering
Now we'll modify files to simulate unauthorized changes.

# Modify content of a file
sudo bash -c 'echo "TAMPERED - Malicious content added" >> /opt/critical_files/config.txt'

# Change permissions on another file
sudo chmod 777 /opt/critical_files/script.sh

# Delete a file
sudo rm /opt/critical_files/data.txt

# Create an unauthorized new file
sudo bash -c 'echo "Backdoor script" > /opt/critical_files/backdoor.sh'
Step 2.6: Detect Tampering
# Run integrity check to detect changes
sudo aide --check
Analyze the output. AIDE will report:

Changed files: Shows what was modified in config.txt
Changed permissions: Shows permission changes on script.sh
Removed files: Shows data.txt was deleted
Added files: Shows backdoor.sh was added
Step 2.7: Generate Detailed Report
# Save detailed report to file
sudo aide --check > /tmp/aide_report.txt 2>&1

# View the report
cat /tmp/aide_report.txt
Step 2.8: Interpret the Report
# Extract only the summary section
grep -A 20 "^Summary:" /tmp/aide_report.txt
The summary shows:

Total number of entries in database
Number of added files
Number of removed files
Number of changed files
Task 3: Create Alert Script
Step 3.1: Create Automated Monitoring Script
Create a script that runs AIDE checks and generates alerts.

# Create monitoring script
sudo nano /usr/local/bin/integrity_monitor.sh
Add the following content:

#!/bin/bash
# Integrity Monitoring Script
# Runs AIDE check and generates alerts

REPORT_FILE="/var/log/aide_check_$(date +%Y%m%d_%H%M%S).log"
ALERT_FILE="/var/log/aide_alerts.log"

# Run AIDE check
echo "Running integrity check at $(date)" | sudo tee -a $ALERT_FILE
sudo aide --check > $REPORT_FILE 2>&1

# Check if changes were detected
if grep -q "found differences" $REPORT_FILE; then
    echo "ALERT: File integrity violations detected!" | sudo tee -a $ALERT_FILE
    echo "Report saved to: $REPORT_FILE" | sudo tee -a $ALERT_FILE
    
    # Extract summary
    grep -A 10 "^Summary:" $REPORT_FILE | sudo tee -a $ALERT_FILE
    
    echo "---" | sudo tee -a $ALERT_FILE
else
    echo "No integrity violations detected." | sudo tee -a $ALERT_FILE
fi
Save and exit (Ctrl+X, Y, Enter).

Step 3.2: Make Script Executable
# Set execute permissions
sudo chmod +x /usr/local/bin/integrity_monitor.sh
Step 3.3: Test Alert Script
# Run the monitoring script
sudo /usr/local/bin/integrity_monitor.sh

# View the alert log
cat /var/log/aide_alerts.log
Step 3.4: View Detailed Report
# List generated reports
ls -lh /var/log/aide_check_*.log

# View the most recent report
sudo cat $(ls -t /var/log/aide_check_*.log | head -1)
Verification
Verify Task 1: Database Creation
# Confirm AIDE database exists and has content
sudo aide --check --config=/etc/aide/aide.conf | head -20
Verify Task 2: Tampering Detection
# Check that changes were detected
grep -c "changed" /tmp/aide_report.txt
Expected: Should show a number greater than 0.

Verify Task 3: Alert Generation
# Verify alert log exists and contains entries
test -f /var/log/aide_alerts.log && echo "Alert log exists" || echo "Alert log missing"

# Count alerts generated
grep -c "ALERT" /var/log/aide_alerts.log
Complete Verification Test
# Restore files to original state
sudo bash -c 'echo "Original content - Configuration file" > /opt/critical_files/config.txt'
sudo chmod 755 /opt/critical_files/script.sh
sudo bash -c 'echo "Original content - Data file" > /opt/critical_files/data.txt'
sudo rm /opt/critical_files/backdoor.sh

# Update database
sudo aide --update
sudo cp /var/lib/aide/aide.db.new /var/lib/aide/aide.db

# Run check - should show no differences
sudo aide --check | grep -i "found NO differences"
If you see "AIDE found NO differences", verification is successful.

Troubleshooting Tips
Issue: AIDE initialization takes too long

Solution: This is normal for first run. AIDE scans many files. Wait 5-10 minutes.
Issue: Permission denied errors

Solution: Ensure you're using sudo for all AIDE commands.
Issue: Database not found error

Solution: Run sudo cp /var/lib/aide/aide.db.new /var/lib/aide/aide.db to activate the database.
Issue: No changes detected after tampering

Solution: Verify the directory is in aide.conf with grep "/opt/critical_files" /etc/aide/aide.conf
